  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    A federal system divides power and neither level can abolish the other. A unitary system delegates from the centre, and a confederation makes the centre the creature of the states.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Size and diversity are the standard reasons, together with the separate colonial administrations of north and south and the regional bargain of the nationalists.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    The exclusive list. The concurrent list is shared, the residual list belongs to the states, and there is no such thing as a local government list.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Defence is exclusive, along with currency, foreign affairs, police and citizenship. Education and agriculture are concurrent, and markets are residual.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Education is concurrent: both the federation and the states may legislate. Currency and foreign affairs are exclusive, and chieftaincy is residual.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Chieftaincy is residual and belongs to the states. Defence, police and citizenship are on the exclusive list.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Section 4(5) of the 1999 Constitution: the federal law prevails and the state law is void only to the extent of the inconsistency. The doctrine is called covering the field.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Covering the field: once the federation has legislated on a concurrent matter, a state law on the same matter yields to the extent of the inconsistency.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Thirty-six states, plus the Federal Capital Territory. Nineteen was the figure after 1976, thirty after 1991, and the FCT is not a state.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    1976, when the decision was taken to move the capital from Lagos to Abuja. The move itself was completed in 1991.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    1996, under Abacha, when six states were created to bring the total to thirty-six.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    774. The other figures are the examiner's near misses.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Section 8 requires a request supported by the people of the area, a two-thirds majority in each house of the National Assembly and in two-thirds of the state houses of assembly.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    The state house of assembly creates local governments, with the consent of the council concerned. States, by contrast, are created by the National Assembly.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Successive creations have appealed to proximity and to minority fears of domination by a large region. The effect, intended or not, has been a stronger centre.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Gowon created twelve states in 1967, abolishing the regions. Nineteen came in 1976, thirty in 1991 and thirty-six in 1996.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    FAAC allocates the Federation Account vertically among the federal, state and local governments and horizontally among the states.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Derivation: the share follows the place from which the revenue is derived. Population, equality and need are the other principles in the formula.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Population, on the argument that a larger population has a larger need. Equality of states favours the smallest units instead.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Equality gives each state the same basic amount on the argument that every state has a minimum cost of government.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Section 14 of the 1999 Constitution requires the composition of federal institutions to reflect the diversity of the country, so that no section is excluded.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    The standard criticism is that a diversity rule can become a quota that overrides qualification. The standard defence is that it prevents exclusion.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    A statutory share of the Federation Account is the main source, supplemented by state allocations, internal revenue and grants.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Markets, tolls, rates, sanitation and local roads are local government functions. Defence, treaties and coinage are federal.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Councils depend on allocations that arrive late and are supervised, and sometimes dissolved, by the state — the classic pair of weaknesses.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    A federal centre has its own powers and acts directly on citizens; in a confederation the centre depends on the states and the states may leave.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Nigeria has one constitution, which divides power. States do not have separate constitutions, cannot make treaties, and the Supreme Court settles disputes between the levels.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    The Supreme Court has original jurisdiction in disputes between the federation and a state, or between states — one of the defining institutions of a federation.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Fiscal federalism is the revenue side of the federal bargain: who taxes, who shares, and on what principle.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    The centre is the only government with a country-wide mandate, and it redistributes revenue. Critics reply that it has become too strong.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    The standard criticism is over-centralisation: states depend on the centre for revenue, and the federal government controls the police and the bulk of the taxes.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Agriculture is concurrent, along with education, health, electricity and industrial development. Citizenship and currency are exclusive, chieftaincy residual.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Markets and motor parks are residual, left to the states and their local governments. Defence, foreign affairs and police are exclusive.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Section 4 establishes the legislative powers of the National Assembly, including the exclusive and concurrent lists. Section 8 deals with state creation.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Residual matters are those not named in the exclusive or concurrent lists, and they fall to the states by default rather than by an express grant.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Each new state adds an administration and a share of the Federation Account, which is why state creation is also a revenue question.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    The FCT is administered by a minister appointed by the President, and it is represented in the National Assembly by a senator and representatives.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Centripetal means tending towards the centre. The abolition of the regions, the creation of more dependent states and the concentration of revenue are the evidence.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    1963 had four regions and a federal parliament; 1999 has thirty-six states and the FCT. Both were federal, but the units and their number differ.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    Disputes between states go to the Supreme Court's original jurisdiction, which is one of the reasons a federation needs a final court.
  ]

]

